{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
[C]From the great Atlantic Ocean to the wide Pacific [F]shore,
To the [G]green old flow'ring mountains, to the ice-bound [C]Labrador
[C]She's long and tall and handsome and known quite well [F]to all.
[G]She's the modern combination called the Wabash Cannonball.[C]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[C]So listen to the jingle, the jumble and the [F]roar
as she [G]glides along the woodlands, through the hills and by the [C]shore.
[C]Hear the mighty rush of the engine, and the lonesome hoboes [F]squall,
While riding [G]through the jungle on the Wabash Cannonball.[C]
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
[C]She came in from Birmingham on a cold and frosty [F]day.
As she [G]rolled into the station, you could hear the people [C]say,
[C]"There’s a gal out there form Tennessee, she’s long, boy, and [F]tall.
[G]She's the modern combination called the Wabash Cannonball."[C]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[C]So listen to the jingle, the jumble and the [F]roar
as she glides [G]along the woodlands, through the hills and by the [C]shore.
[C]Hear the mighty rush of the engine, and the lonesome hoboes [F]squall,
While [G]riding through the jungle on the Wabash [C]Cannonball.
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Now the [C]Eastern states are dandy, so the all the people [F]say,
From [G]New York to Saint Louis and Chicago by the [C]way,
From the [C]lakes of Minnehaha where the laughing waters [F]fall,
No [G]change in standard gauging on the Wabash Cannonball.[C]
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
[C]So listen to the jingle, the jumble and the [F]roar
as she glides [G]along the woodlands, through the hills and by the [C]shore.
[C]Hear the mighty rush of the engine, and the lonesome hoboes [F]squall,
While riding [G]through the jungle on the Wabash Cannonball.[C]
{end_of_chorus}
